      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
          "content": "To examine the influence of maternal pre-pregnancy body mass index (BMI) on the rates of recurrent spontaneous preterm birth (SPTB) in women receiving 17α-hydroxyprogesterone caproate (17P)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "Retrospective analysis of a cohort of 6253 women with a singleton gestation and prior SPTB enrolled in 17P home administration program between 16.0 and 26.9 weeks. Data were grouped by pre-pregnancy BMI (lean <18.5\u2009kg/m(2), normal 18.5-24.9\u2009kg/m(2), overweight 25-29.9\u2009kg/m(2) and obese ≥30.0\u2009kg/m(2)). Delivery outcomes were compared using χ(2) and Kruskal-Wallis tests with statistical significance set at p\u2009<\u20090.05."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "SPTB<28 weeks was significantly lower in normal weight women. Rates of recurrent SPTB<37 weeks were highest in the group with BMI<18.5\u2009kg/m(2). Lean gravidas were younger, more likely to smoke, and less likely to be African-American than those with normal or increased BMI. In logistic regression, after controlling for race and prior preterm birth <28 weeks, the risk of SPTB<37 weeks decreased 2% for every additional 1\u2009kg/m(2) increase in BMI."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "Recurrent spontaneous preterm delivery<37 weeks in patients on 17P is more common in lean women (BMI<18.5\u2009kg/m(2)), and less common in obese women (BMI ≥30\u2009kg/m(2)) suggesting that the current recommended dosing of 17\u2009P is adequate for women with higher BMI."
        }
      ]},
